Let the MOV demuxer read the initial view that Google's first spherical box states.

Version 1 of Google's spherical video metadata is an XML document in a uuid box inside the track,
and besides the projection and the stereo mode it can state where the viewer first looks, with
InitialViewHeadingDegrees, InitialViewPitchDegrees and InitialViewRollDegrees. The demuxer found
each element with av_stristr and handed what it found straight to strtol, but that points at the
opening tag's '<' rather than at the number after it, so strtol found no digits and every angle
read as 0 while the projection and the stereo mode read correctly.

The demuxer now reads the number after each opening tag. The specification draws its initial
view on the axes spherical.h draws for AVSphericalMapping, heading from -180 at the left to 180
at the right and pitch from -90 at the bottom to 90 at the top, and its roll of 90 turns the up
vector to the right, where spherical.h tilts the view to the right for a positive roll. So
heading becomes yaw, pitch stays pitch and roll stays roll with their signs as they are, as this
demuxer already reads sv3d's pose. VLC's MP4 reader skips these
angles, and it reads the same three from a photo sphere's metadata with the signs it gives sv3d's
pose. The specification states each angle as an integer, and a fraction is kept too, rounded to the
nearest step of 16.16. The number is read with av_sscanf, because strtod follows the process
locale, and a JVM sets that from the user's, so in a German one it read 15.5 as 15. An element
that holds no number, or one that 16.16 cannot carry, leaves its angle at 0 as before.

Applied by BuildFFmpegTask before configure, after 0009. Fixes yuroyami/KiteFFmpeg#161.

diff --git a/libavformat/mov.c b/libavformat/mov.c
index 9b742ac..2d6ea52 100644
--- a/libavformat/mov.c
+++ b/libavformat/mov.c
@@ -7514,6 +7514,23 @@ static void mov_merge_vexu(MOVStreamContext *sc)
     av_freep(&sc->vexu_stereo3d);
 }
 
+/**
+ * Read the angle in degrees that an element of the spherical uuid box holds, as 16.16 fixed
+ * point. The number follows the element's opening tag. Version 1 of Google's specification
+ * states an integer, and a fraction is kept too, read the same way in every locale. An element
+ * that is missing, holds no number or holds one that 16.16 cannot carry leaves the angle as it
+ * was.
+ */
+static void mov_parse_uuid_spherical_angle(const char *xml, const char *tag, int32_t *angle)
+{
+    const char *val = av_stristr(xml, tag);
+    double degrees;
+
+    if (val && av_sscanf(val + strlen(tag), "%lf", &degrees) == 1 &&
+        isfinite(degrees) && fabs(degrees) <= 32767.0)
+        *angle = lrint(degrees * (1 << 16));
+}
+
 static int mov_parse_uuid_spherical(MOVStreamContext *sc, AVIOContext *pb, size_t len)
 {
     int ret = 0;
@@ -7560,16 +7577,13 @@ static int mov_parse_uuid_spherical(MOVStreamContext *sc, AVIOContext *pb, size_
             sc->stereo3d->type = mode;
         }
 
-        /* orientation */
-        val = av_stristr(buffer, "<GSpherical:InitialViewHeadingDegrees>");
-        if (val)
-            sc->spherical->yaw = strtol(val, NULL, 10) * (1 << 16);
-        val = av_stristr(buffer, "<GSpherical:InitialViewPitchDegrees>");
-        if (val)
-            sc->spherical->pitch = strtol(val, NULL, 10) * (1 << 16);
-        val = av_stristr(buffer, "<GSpherical:InitialViewRollDegrees>");
-        if (val)
-            sc->spherical->roll = strtol(val, NULL, 10) * (1 << 16);
+        /* orientation, which the specification draws on the same axes as AVSphericalMapping */
+        mov_parse_uuid_spherical_angle(buffer, "<GSpherical:InitialViewHeadingDegrees>",
+                                       &sc->spherical->yaw);
+        mov_parse_uuid_spherical_angle(buffer, "<GSpherical:InitialViewPitchDegrees>",
+                                       &sc->spherical->pitch);
+        mov_parse_uuid_spherical_angle(buffer, "<GSpherical:InitialViewRollDegrees>",
+                                       &sc->spherical->roll);
     }
 
